<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Charles XII of Sweden</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. <a href="/years/1697">1697</a>–<a href="/years/1718">1718</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Charles XII</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Charles XII was king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> from 1697 to 1718 and a commander of great tactical skill. He won notable victories such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narva">Narva</a> in 1700 but was decisively defeated by Peter I of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-poltava">Poltava</a> in 1709, after which he spent years in Ottoman exile. His death in 1718 marked the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swedish-empire">Swedish Empire</a> as a great power.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/charles-xii-of-sweden.jpg" alt="Charles XII of Sweden" width="320" height="402" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Workshop of Hyacinthe Rigaud · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Copy_Charles_XII_-_Nationalmuseum_-_17886.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>When Mazepa joined <b>Charles XII</b> on 28 October 1708, Peter I had Menshikov burn Baturyn, killing up to 15,000 people on 13 November, executed over 900 officers at Lebedyn, destroyed the Zaporozhian Sich in May 1709 and beat the Swedes at the Battle of Poltava on 8 July…</blockquote><a href="/empire/cossack-hetmanate">Cossack Hetmanate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Under Danish kings Norway supplied sailors and soldiers, and its border fortress of Fredriksten saw the death of <b>Charles XII of Sweden</b> in 1718.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-norway">Kingdom of Norway · Military</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Charles XII</b> won at Narva in 1700 but was crushed by Peter the Great at the Battle of Poltava in 1709 and killed at Fredriksten in Norway in 1718.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-sweden">Kingdom of Sweden · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The imperial proclamation was made possible by the defeat of <b>Charles XII</b>'s Sweden, previously the dominant Baltic power, and by the international recognition that followed, which established Russia for the first time as an undeniable participant in the European state system.</blockquote><a href="/empire/russian-empire">Russian Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Charles XII</b> (r. 1697–1718) was the high point of personal Swedish generalship: a commander of great tactical skill whose victories at Narva (1700) and Holowczyn (1708) against numerically superior opponents rank among the finest tactical performances of the early eighteenth century.</blockquote><a href="/empire/swedish-empire">Swedish Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Peter I took Azov in 1696 with a newly built fleet, was defeated by <b>Charles XII of Sweden</b> at Narva in 1700, rebuilt his army and won decisively at the Battle of Poltava in 1709, and later at sea off Hangö in 1714.</blockquote><a href="/empire/tsardom-of-russia">Tsardom of Russia · Military</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in The Royal Court Parish, in what is now <a href="/countries/sweden">Sweden</a>. Died in Halden, in what is now <a href="/countries/norway">Norway</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=59.33,18.07&amp;year=1707" aria-label="The Royal Court Parish on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4931 170 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Charles XII of Sweden"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5381 440h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5381 440h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=59.33,18.07&amp;year=1707">What was here in 1707? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
